  <p>This function supports <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/List#Strided_lists" target="_blank">Strided Lists</a>.</p>
  <h2>Specification</h2>
  <p>The sort order is affected by type. For strings and keys, it is case sensitive and sorts by Unicode character code.</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat

ll.ListSortStrided({"a", "á", "B", "C", "d", "e"}, 1, 0, true)  -- returns ["B", "C", "a", "d", "e", "á"]`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`llListSortStrided(["a", "á", "B", "C", "d", "e"], 1, 0, TRUE) // returns ["B", "C", "a", "d", "e", "á"]`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <p>For ascending sort, each type is sorted individually and then feathered to have the same order of types.</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat

ll.ListSortStrided({1, "C", 3, "A", 2, "B"}, 1, 0, true)  -- returns [1, "A", 2, "B", 3, "C"]

ll.ListSortStrided({1, 3, 2, "C", "A", "B"}, 1, 0, true)  -- returns [1, 2, 3, "A", "B", "C"]

ll.ListSortStrided({1, "C", 3, "A", 2, "B"}, 2, 0, true)  -- returns [1, "C", 2, "B", 3, "A"]

ll.ListSortStrided({1, "C", 3, "A", 2, "B"}, 2, 1, true)  -- returns [3, "A", 2, "B", 1, "C"]`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`llListSortStrided([1, "C", 3, "A", 2, "B"], 1, 0, TRUE) // returns [1, "A", 2, "B", 3, "C"]

llListSortStrided([1, 3, 2, "C", "A", "B"], 1, 0, TRUE) // returns [1, 2, 3, "A", "B", "C"]

llListSortStrided([1, "C", 3, "A", 2, "B"], 2, 0, TRUE) // returns [1, "C", 2, "B", 3, "A"]

llListSortStrided([1, "C", 3, "A", 2, "B"], 2, 1, TRUE) // returns [3, "A", 2, "B", 1, "C"]`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <h2>Caveats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It uses the same unoptimized selection sort algorithm as llListSort, which is an algorithm with a Big O of N². A <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/JIRA" target="_blank">JIRA</a> issue exists to improve this function, <a href="https://jira.secondlife.com/browse/SVC-2988" target="_blank">SVC-2988</a>.</li>
    <li>Originally the wiki stated that non-zero values for the &quot;ascending&quot; parameter would produce an ascending sort. That was incorrect. For this function, the value must be exactly 1 (or TRUE) for an ascending sort.</li>
    <li><a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/Vector" target="_blank">Vectors</a> are sorted by magnitude. <a href="https://jira.secondlife.com/browse/SVC-5643" target="_blank">SVC-5643</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/Rotation" target="_blank">Rotations</a> are not sorted in any meaningful order. If a list containing only rotations is sorted in ascending order, it will be returned unchanged.</li>
    <li><p>For descending sort, if there are mixed types, the final order is deterministic (the same input will always produce the same output) but it can be completely useless.</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat



ll.ListSortStrided({2, "B", "C", 3, 1, "A"}, 1, 0, false)  -- returns ["A", 3, 1, "C", "B", 2]
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`
llListSortStrided([2, "B", "C", 3, 1, "A"], 1, 0, FALSE) // returns ["A", 3, 1, "C", "B", 2]
`} lang="lsl" /></div>


<p>If there are no mixed types, however, the descending sort works just fine.</p></li>
    <li>When the stride is greater than 1, if the list length is not a multiple of the stride, the list will be returned unchanged.</li>
    <li>stride_index must be less than stride and greater than or equal to -stride, otherwise an empty list is returned.</li>
    <li><p>When strings contain numbers, the numbers are still sorted left-to-right like any other character, which may not necessarily match numeric order:</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat



ll.ListSortStrided({"127", "3", "25"}, 1, 0, true)  -- returns ["127", "25", "3"] because the 1 in 127 is before the 2 in 25 which is before the 3
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`
llListSortStrided(["127", "3", "25"], 1, 0, TRUE) // returns ["127", "25", "3"] because the 1 in 127 is before the 2 in 25 which is before the 3
`} lang="lsl" /></div>


<p>To sort them in numeric order, numbers in strings can be padded with zeros:</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat



ll.ListSortStrided({"127", "003", "025"}, 1, 0, true)  -- returns ["003", "025", "127"]
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`
llListSortStrided(["127", "003", "025"], 1, 0, TRUE) // returns ["003", "025", "127"]
`} lang="lsl" /></div>


    <ul>
      <li>This order differs from the order of items in a prim&#39;s inventory, which is &quot;natural order&quot; (e.g &quot;New Script 2&quot; is sorted before &quot;New Script 11&quot;).</li>
    </ul>
  
    </li>
    <li><p>The sorting algorithm is not <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorting_algorithm#Stability" target="_blank">stable</a>. Thus you cannot use 2 sorts on different columns to sort by multiple keys:</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat


local function main()  -- THIS DOES NOT WORK AS A MULTI-KEY SORT
    local input = {1, "b", 0, "c", 1, "a", 0, "b", 1, "c", 0, "a"}
    local partialSort = ll.ListSortStrided(input, 2, 1, true)
    ll.OwnerSay("partialSort = " .. ll.List2Json(JSON_ARRAY, partialSort))
    local fullSort = ll.ListSortStrided(partialSort, 2, 0, true)
    ll.OwnerSay("fullSort = " .. ll.List2Json(JSON_ARRAY, fullSort))
end


main()
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`
default { state_entry() { // THIS DOES NOT WORK AS A MULTI-KEY SORT
    list input = [1, "b", 0, "c", 1, "a", 0, "b", 1, "c", 0, "a"];
    list partialSort = llListSortStrided(input, 2, 1, TRUE);
    llOwnerSay("partialSort = " + llList2Json(JSON_ARRAY, partialSort));
    list fullSort = llListSortStrided(partialSort, 2, 0, TRUE);
    llOwnerSay("fullSort = " + llList2Json(JSON_ARRAY, fullSort));
} }
`} lang="lsl" /></div>


<p>This gives the following output (errors bolded):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>partialSort = [1,&quot;a&quot;,0,&quot;a&quot;,<b>0,</b>&quot;b&quot;,<b>1,</b>&quot;b&quot;,<b>1,</b>&quot;c&quot;,<b>0,</b>&quot;c&quot;]</li>
      <li>fullSort = [0,&quot;a&quot;,0,&quot;b&quot;,0,&quot;c&quot;,1,<b>&quot;b&quot;,</b>1,<b>&quot;c&quot;,</b>1,<b>&quot;a&quot;</b>]</li>
    </ul>
  
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Examples</h2>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat

local score_board = {"Awesome", "Resident", 200, "Star", "Marxman", 999, "Happy2", "Survive", 1}
local function main()
    ll.OwnerSay("Unsorted: " .. ll.DumpList2String(score_board, ","))
    score_board = ll.ListSortStrided(scoreboard, 3, 0, true)
    ll.OwnerSay("Sort by first names: " .. ll.DumpList2String(numbers, ","))
    -- Object: Sort by first names: Awesome,Resident,200,Happy2,Survive,1,Star,Marxman,999

    score_board = ll.ListSortStrided(scoreboard, 3, 1, true)
    ll.OwnerSay("Sort by last names: " .. ll.DumpList2String(numbers, ","))
    -- Object: Sort by first names: Star,Marxman,999,Awesome,Resident,200,Happy2,Survive,1

    score_board = ll.ListSortStrided(scoreboard, 3, 2, true)
    ll.OwnerSay("Sort by last names: " .. ll.DumpList2String(numbers, ","))
-- Object: Sort by first names: ,Happy2,Survive,1,Awesome,Resident,200,Star,Marxman,999

end

main()
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`list score_board = ["Awesome", "Resident", 200, "Star", "Marxman", 999, "Happy2", "Survive", 1];
default
{
    state_entry()
    {
        llOwnerSay("Unsorted: " + llDumpList2String(score_board, ","));
        score_board = llListSortStrided(scoreboard, 3, 0, TRUE);
        llOwnerSay("Sort by first names: " + llDumpList2String(numbers, ","));
        // Object: Sort by first names: Awesome,Resident,200,Happy2,Survive,1,Star,Marxman,999

        score_board = llListSortStrided(scoreboard, 3, 1, TRUE);
        llOwnerSay("Sort by last names: " + llDumpList2String(numbers, ","));
        // Object: Sort by first names: Star,Marxman,999,Awesome,Resident,200,Happy2,Survive,1

        score_board = llListSortStrided(scoreboard, 3, 2, TRUE);
        llOwnerSay("Sort by last names: " + llDumpList2String(numbers, ","));
        // Object: Sort by first names: ,Happy2,Survive,1,Awesome,Resident,200,Star,Marxman,999

    }
}`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <p>llListSort and llListSortStrided really only works on items of the same type. It will work on lists that hold diverse data types -- to be clear, it won&#39;t blow up your script -- but the results returned are usually meaningless.</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat

local mylist = {"brown", vector(0.000000, 0.000000, 0.000000), "house", 17.005, 100, "cat", vector(3.000000, 3.000000, 3.000000), 39}
local tmplist = ll.ListSortStrided(mylist, 1, 0, true)
ll.Say(0, ll.List2CSV(tmplist))`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`list mylist = ["brown", <0.000000, 0.000000, 0.000000>, "house", 17.005, 100, "cat", <3.000000, 3.000000, 3.000000>, 39];
list tmplist = llListSortStrided(mylist, 1, 0, TRUE);
llSay(0, llList2CSV(tmplist));`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <p>This returns in chat:</p>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`brown, <0.000000, 0.000000, 0.000000>, cat, 17.004999, 39, house, <3.000000, 3.000000, 3.000000>, 100`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <p>The same ordered in descending order returns even more meaningless results:</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat

local mylist = {"brown", vector(0.000000, 0.000000, 0.000000), "house", 17.005, 100, "cat", vector(3.000000, 3.000000, 3.000000), 39}
local tmplist = ll.ListSortStrided(mylist, 1, 0, false)
ll.Say(0, ll.List2CSV(tmplist))`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`list mylist = ["brown", <0.000000, 0.000000, 0.000000>, "house", 17.005, 100, "cat", <3.000000, 3.000000, 3.000000>, 39];
list tmplist = llListSortStrided(mylist, 1, 0, FALSE);
llSay(0, llList2CSV(tmplist));`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <p>returns in chat:</p>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`39, <3.000000, 3.000000, 3.000000>, cat, 100, 17.004999, house, <0.000000, 0.000000, 0.000000>, brown`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <h3>Utilizing the Results</h3>
  <p>It&#39;s important to note that the source list that you are sorting will remain unchanged. Instead, a new, sorted list will be produced. So, it&#39;s important that you capture this with a variable (unless you are acting directly on the results.)</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat

ll.ListSortStrided(myList, 1, 0, true)  -- You've wasted cpu time; you didn't capture the results

local newlist = ll.ListSortStrided(myList, 1, 0, true)  -- Okay. You've captured the results.

ll.Say(0, ll.List2CSV(ll.ListSortStrided(myList, 1, 0, true)))  -- No need to capture, using the results right away.`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`llListSortStrided(myList, 1, 0, TRUE); // You've wasted cpu time; you didn't capture the results

list newlist = llListSortStrided(myList, 1, 0, TRUE);// Okay. You've captured the results.

llSay(0,llList2CSV(llListSortStrided(myList, 1, 0, TRUE))); // No need to capture, using the results right away.`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <h3><b>Stride</b> parameter</h3>
  <p>Most times, you will want to set &quot;integer stride&quot; to 1 (0 also works) to tell it to sort each item in the list on its own basis. (If you are working with a strided list, though, see the special section below on sorting strides.)</p>
  <h3>Sort Order</h3>
  <p>Setting the parameter &quot;integer ascending&quot; to <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/TRUE" target="_blank">TRUE</a> returns a sorted list that is in ascending order.</p>
  <p>For example: [&quot;Apples&quot;, &quot;Bananas&quot;, &quot;Oranges&quot;]</p>
  <p>Setting the parameter &quot;integer ascending&quot; to <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/FALSE" target="_blank">FALSE</a> returns a sorted list that is in descending order.</p>
  <p>For example: [&quot;Oranges&quot;, &quot;Bananas&quot;, &quot;Apples&quot;]</p>
  <h3>Sorting Strided Lists</h3>
  <p>If you have a <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/List#Strided_lists" target="_blank">strided list</a>, in which you are keeping related pieces of data together in chunks, letting each list element sort on its own basis would be disastrous.</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`local demographics = {"John Adams", "male", "2007-06-22", "Shirley Bassey", "female", "2005-11-02", "Matt Damon", "male", "2008-05-19"}
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`list demographics = ["John Adams", "male", "2007-06-22", "Shirley Bassey", "female", "2005-11-02", "Matt Damon", "male", "2008-05-19"];`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <p>Bad Example</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat

local tmplist_1 = ll.ListSortStrided(demographics, 1, 0, true)
--tmplist_1 == ["2005-11-02", "2007-06-22", "2008-05-19", "John Adams", "Matt Damon", "Shirley Bassey", "female", "male", "male"]
--The strides have been destroyed, the sorted data is now useless`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`list tmplist_1 = llListSortStrided(demographics, 1, 0, TRUE);
//tmplist_1 == ["2005-11-02", "2007-06-22", "2008-05-19", "John Adams", "Matt Damon", "Shirley Bassey", "female", "male", "male"]
//The strides have been destroyed, the sorted data is now useless`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <p>Good Example</p>
  <p>Instead, because you have the data grouped (aka &quot;strided&quot;) in sets of 3, you need to do this:</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`ll = llcompat

local tmplist_2 = ll.ListSortStrided(demographics, 3, 0, true)
--templist_2 = ["John Adams", "male", "2007-06-22", "Matt Damon", "male", "2008-05-19", "Shirley Bassey", "female", "2005-11-02"]`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`list tmplist_2 = llListSortStrided(demographics, 3, 0, TRUE);
//templist_2 = ["John Adams", "male", "2007-06-22", "Matt Damon", "male", "2008-05-19", "Shirley Bassey", "female", "2005-11-02"]`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <h2>Deep Notes</h2>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th>Signature</th>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`
function list llListSortStrided( list src, integer stride, integer stride_index, integer ascending );
`} lang="lsl" /></div>

</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

</DocsLayout>
      